.TH "ARI" "1" "October 2026" "ari 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
ari - keep SSH hosts in one place and
generate ssh config from them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]ari\f[R] [\f[B]-i\f[R]
\f[I]INV\f[R]] \f[I]COMMAND\f[R]
[\f[I]ARGS\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]ari ls\f[R] [\f[B]--search\f[R]
\f[I]TEXT\f[R]] [\f[B]--group\f[R]
\f[I]GROUP\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]ari show\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]ari import ssh\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
[\f[B]--no-ansible\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]ari export\f[R] [\f[B]ssh\f[R] |
\f[B]ansible\f[R]] [\f[B]--force\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\f[B]ari\f[R] keeps one record per SSH
host in JSON inventories and writes ssh
config files from them.
Each inventory, such as
\f[I]personal\f[R] or \f[I]work\f[R], is
declared in \f[B]config.toml\f[R] with
its own export targets.
.PP
Names and aliases are unique across all
inventories, compared case-insensitively
the way ssh compares them.
Every generated file lands in one ssh
namespace where the first match wins, so
a duplicate would silently shadow a
host.
.PP
Generated files are output, not
something to edit.
\f[B]ari\f[R] records a hash of every
file it writes and refuses to overwrite
one that changed since.
.PP
With no arguments, \f[B]ari\f[R] prints
help.
.SH THE NAME
.PP
\f[B]ari\f[R] is Turkish for bee.
Bees spend their days building the hive
and stocking it, and that is the whole
job here: keep every host in one place,
and build ssh config and Ansible
inventory from it.
.PP
The name started with a plush hive.
My son has one with five bees, and he
loves it.
One of the bees lives on my desk.
.PP
In Hebrew, ari means lion.
When Samson came back to the lion he had
killed, he found bees and honey inside
it:
.RS
.PP
And after a time he returned to take
her, and he turned aside to see the
carcase of the lion: and, behold, there
was a swarm of bees and honey in the
carcase of the lion.
.PP
Judges 14:8 (KJV)
.RE
.PP
Out of the strong came forth sweetness.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS ls
.PP
List hosts across every inventory, or
only the one named with \f[B]-i\f[R].
User and port show effective values,
with inventory defaults filled in.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(24.5n) lw(45.5n).
T{
Option
T}@T{
Meaning
T}
_
T{
\f[B]--search\f[R] \f[I]TEXT\f[R]
T}@T{
Only hosts with \f[I]TEXT\f[R] in any
field, case-insensitive
T}
T{
\f[B]--group\f[R] \f[I]GROUP\f[R]
T}@T{
Only hosts in \f[I]GROUP\f[R]
T}
.TE
.SS show \f[I]NAME\f[R]
.PP
Show one host, found by name or alias in
any inventory, with every field.
Values inherited from inventory defaults
are marked \f[B](default)\f[R].
.SS import ssh \f[I]FILE\f[R]
.PP
Read the Host blocks in \f[I]FILE\f[R]
into one inventory.
The first token on a Host line becomes
the name and the rest become aliases.
\f[B]HostName\f[R], \f[B]User\f[R],
\f[B]Port\f[R] and
\f[B]IdentityFile\f[R] become fields,
\f[B]IdentitiesOnly yes\f[R] is implied
by a key, and every other keyword is
kept as an ssh option, in order.
.PP
Pattern Host blocks, Match blocks,
Include lines and options outside any
Host block are skipped with a warning.
When a keyword repeats inside a block,
the first value is kept, as ssh does.
.PP
A host already in the inventory is
merged: new aliases and options are
added.
A different HostName, User, Port or
IdentityFile is reported as a conflict
and left alone.
A host whose name or alias already
belongs to another inventory is refused.
.PP
Importing into an empty inventory sets
its default user and key to the values
most hosts share, and each host stores
only what differs.
Import records the hash of
\f[I]FILE\f[R], so exporting over that
same file afterwards passes the guard.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(24.5n) lw(45.5n).
T{
Option
T}@T{
Meaning
T}
_
T{
\f[B]--no-ansible\f[R]
T}@T{
Mark imported hosts as ssh only, never
exported to Ansible
T}
.TE
.SS export [ssh | ansible]
.PP
Write every target of every inventory,
or one kind of output, or one inventory
with \f[B]-i\f[R].
Validation and the guard check run
first; if any fails, nothing is written.
.PP
Ansible export is not implemented yet.
The target is accepted and skipped with
a note.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(24.5n) lw(45.5n).
T{
Option
T}@T{
Meaning
T}
_
T{
\f[B]--force\f[R]
T}@T{
Overwrite files that were edited since
the last export, or never written by
\f[B]ari\f[R]
T}
.TE
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(24.5n) lw(45.5n).
T{
Option
T}@T{
Meaning
T}
_
T{
\f[B]-i\f[R], \f[B]--inventory\f[R]
\f[I]INV\f[R]
T}@T{
Inventory to act on; accepted before or
after the command
T}
T{
\f[B]-h\f[R], \f[B]--help\f[R]
T}@T{
Show help for \f[B]ari\f[R] or for one
command
T}
T{
\f[B]--version\f[R]
T}@T{
Show the version
T}
.TE
.PP
\f[B]import\f[R] writes to one
inventory: \f[B]-i\f[R], then
\f[B]ARI_INVENTORY\f[R], then
\f[B]default\f[R] from config.toml.
\f[B]ls\f[R] and \f[B]export\f[R] cover
every inventory unless \f[B]-i\f[R]
narrows them.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.PP
\f[B]config.toml\f[R] declares the
inventories and where each one exports.
\f[B]ari\f[R] reads it and never writes
it.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
default = \[dq]personal\[dq]

[inventories.personal]
ssh = \[dq]\[ti]/.ssh/config.d/10-personal.conf\[dq]

[inventories.work]
file = \[dq]\[ti]/work/infra/ari/work.json\[dq]
ssh = \[dq]\[ti]/.ssh/config.d/20-work.conf\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(24.5n) lw(45.5n).
T{
Key
T}@T{
Meaning
T}
_
T{
\f[B]default\f[R]
T}@T{
Inventory used by \f[B]import\f[R] when
\f[B]-i\f[R] and \f[B]ARI_INVENTORY\f[R]
are unset
T}
T{
\f[V]inventories.NAME.file\f[R]
T}@T{
Inventory data; defaults to
\f[V]NAME.json\f[R] beside config.toml
T}
T{
\f[V]inventories.NAME.ssh\f[R]
T}@T{
Generated ssh config; absolute or
starting with \f[B]\[ti]\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[V]inventories.NAME.ansible\f[R]
T}@T{
Reserved for Ansible export
T}
.TE
.PP
\f[B]\[ti]/.ssh/config\f[R] pulls the
generated files in with one line, placed
before any Host block:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Include config.d/*.conf
\f[R]
.fi
.SH INVENTORY FILES
.PP
Each inventory is one JSON file.
Hosts store only what differs from the
inventory \f[B]defaults\f[R].
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
{
  \[dq]version\[dq]: 1,
  \[dq]last_updated\[dq]: \[dq]2026-10-02T21:14:00+01:00\[dq],
  \[dq]defaults\[dq]: {\[dq]user\[dq]: \[dq]tom\[dq], \[dq]ssh_key\[dq]: \[dq]\[ti]/.ssh/id-ed25519\[dq]},
  \[dq]groups\[dq]: {},
  \[dq]hosts\[dq]: [
    {
      \[dq]name\[dq]: \[dq]nas\[dq],
      \[dq]hostname\[dq]: \[dq]192.0.2.254\[dq],
      \[dq]aliases\[dq]: [\[dq]storage\[dq]],
      \[dq]user\[dq]: \[dq]root\[dq],
      \[dq]last_updated\[dq]: \[dq]2026-10-02T21:14:00+01:00\[dq]
    }
  ]
}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(24.5n) lw(45.5n).
T{
Field
T}@T{
Meaning
T}
_
T{
\f[B]name\f[R]
T}@T{
ssh alias; required
T}
T{
\f[B]hostname\f[R]
T}@T{
Address ssh connects to; required
T}
T{
\f[B]aliases\f[R]
T}@T{
More names on the Host line
T}
T{
\f[B]user\f[R], \f[B]port\f[R],
\f[B]ssh_key\f[R]
T}@T{
Override the inventory defaults
T}
T{
\f[B]ssh_options\f[R]
T}@T{
Any other ssh_config keywords, written
in order
T}
T{
\f[B]notes\f[R]
T}@T{
Written as a comment above the Host
block
T}
T{
\f[B]groups\f[R], \f[B]reasons\f[R]
T}@T{
Ansible group membership; used once
Ansible export exists
T}
T{
\f[B]ansible\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]false\f[R] keeps the host out of
Ansible export
T}
.TE
.PP
A file that fails to parse stops
\f[B]ari\f[R] with the path and the
error.
It is never treated as empty.
.SH GENERATED SSH CONFIG
.PP
Hosts are sorted by name.
Each block gets \f[B]HostName\f[R] and
\f[B]User\f[R], \f[B]Port\f[R] when it
isn\[aq]t 22, and \f[B]IdentityFile\f[R]
with \f[B]IdentitiesOnly yes\f[R] when a
key is set, followed by the host\[aq]s
other options.
There are no \f[V]Host *\f[R] blocks:
they ignore file boundaries, and
\f[B]IdentityFile\f[R] accumulates
across matching blocks, so a default in
one file would offer its key to every
host.
.PP
Every write goes to \f[V]FILE.tmp\f[R]
beside the target and is then renamed
over it.
\f[V]Include config.d/*.conf\f[R] never
matches the \f[V].tmp\f[R], so ssh never
reads a half-written file.
.SH FILES
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(24.5n) lw(45.5n).
T{
File
T}@T{
Contents
T}
_
T{
\f[V]config.toml\f[R]
T}@T{
Inventories and their targets
T}
T{
\f[V]NAME.json\f[R]
T}@T{
Inventory data, one file per inventory
T}
T{
\f[V]exports.json\f[R]
T}@T{
Hashes of exported files, for the guard
T}
.TE
.PP
\f[V]config.toml\f[R] and the
inventories live in
\f[V]\[ti]/.config/ari/\f[R], and
\f[V]exports.json\f[R] in
\f[V]\[ti]/.local/state/ari/\f[R].
\f[B]XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R] and
\f[B]XDG_STATE_HOME\f[R] move them.
Losing the state file only means the
guard refuses existing targets until the
next import or \f[B]--force\f[R].
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(24.5n) lw(45.5n).
T{
Variable
T}@T{
Effect
T}
_
T{
\f[B]ARI_INVENTORY\f[R]
T}@T{
Inventory for \f[B]import\f[R] when
\f[B]-i\f[R] is not given
T}
T{
\f[B]XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R]
T}@T{
Where config.toml and inventories live
T}
T{
\f[B]XDG_STATE_HOME\f[R]
T}@T{
Where the guard state lives
T}
.TE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
\f[B]0\f[R] on success.
\f[B]1\f[R] on an error, a validation
failure, a guard refusal or an import
conflict.
\f[B]2\f[R] on a usage error.
\f[B]130\f[R] when interrupted.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Take over an existing ssh config file,
then regenerate it:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
ari import ssh \[ti]/.ssh/config.d/10-personal.conf -i personal
ari export
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Find every host on a subnet:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
ari ls --search 192.0.2.
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Accept a file after reviewing a hand
edit:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
ari export --force
\f[R]
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\f[B]ssh\f[R](1),
\f[B]ssh_config\f[R](5)
